      "writtenOn": "2014-08-14T14:53:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, I looked at this and don\u0027t see any potential problems with the way it is. It can be called from interrupt context, in which case it would be better to leave this function as early as possible, and it can be called from host command task. But,  the task doesn\u0027t do anything but wait for host commands and process them, so no concern for exiting from here before finished since the host command task normally will just wait to receive the next command, which can\u0027t come until this one is finished anyway.\n\nOne style comment though, brackets aren\u0027t needed around single line if\u0027s.",
      "parentUuid": "36901b99_a928b108",
      "revId": "54db972fc98fe703c33e7fcc2d1d0d4847a045b9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "36901b99_b8febd43",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/i2c-stm32f0.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 251,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-08-13T18:42:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m wondering if we should clear the STM32_I2C_ISR_TXIS bit from i2c_isr here so that we don\u0027t stuff an 0xec into the transmitter queue. My current board that uses this will consume these bytes until the end, but I\u0027m not sure about other users. Alec, can you try this patch on samus and see if it behaves?",
      "revId": "54db972fc98fe703c33e7fcc2d1d0d4847a045b9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "766ff376_72886e99",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/i2c-stm32f0.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 251,
      "author": {
        "id": 1103909
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-08-14T01:56:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think it matters because next time it is our turn transmit, we clear the transmit buffer first (line 232).\n\nAlso, once we get here, the master has issued a STOP on the bus, so I don\u0027t think your board is any different from any other. If we have a STOP condition and a TXIS at the same time, then EC will disable TXIS interrupt, but then stuff \u00270xec\u0027 in the buffer, but the host will never read it because it has stopped clocking out, so same behavior for all hosts.\n\nI will test tomorrow. I don\u0027t have an easy way to test error case where you use the wrong protocol, but I can test returning an error from a host command function, which I think does the same thing.",
      "parentUuid": "36901b99_b8febd43",
      "revId": "54db972fc98fe703c33e7fcc2d1d0d4847a045b9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b626cb4d_2d07e1aa",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/i2c-stm32f0.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 251,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-08-14T13:00:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, if we stuff that byte into the buffer does it get clocked out on the subsequent read? Or is the fifo actually cleared? That\u0027s why I suggested it was easier to just knock down that bit for sure here. Granted, this worked on ryu, but I didn\u0027t want to cause any errant 0xec bytes.",
      "parentUuid": "766ff376_72886e99",
      "revId": "54db972fc98fe703c33e7fcc2d1d0d4847a045b9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "766ff376_86bb5bc4",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/i2c-stm32f0.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 251,
      "author": {
        "id": 1103909
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-08-14T14:53:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There isn\u0027t a FIFO, the buffer is just a single byte.\nThe tx buffer gets cleared by setting ISR register TXE bit: \"This bit can be written to ‘1’ by software in order to flush the transmit data register I2Cx_TXDR.\"\n\nAlso, how would you clear TXIS interrupt? You can\u0027t clear it by writing a 1, the only way I see to clear it is to write to the transmit buffer (TXDR). So, I don\u0027t think there is any concern for getting errant 0xec bytes on the next transfer.",
      "parentUuid": "b626cb4d_2d07e1aa",
      "revId": "54db972fc98fe703c33e7fcc2d1d0d4847a045b9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    }
